## 12:10 Carry the Explanation Forward

We can now answer the main question from the beginning of the lesson.

> **How can we find out exactly what a piece of text contains and then prepare it consistently for later AI work?**

We begin with the unchanged source.

We inspect it to reveal hidden whitespace and stored character identities.

We then decide which differences matter for our purpose.

After choosing the rules, we apply them consistently and test the result with more than one example.

The overall process is:

```text
source text
→ inspect what is stored
→ identify important differences
→ choose rules that fit the purpose
→ apply the rules
→ test the result
→ prepared text
```

The most important distinction is this:

> **Identification tells us what is present. Preparation decides what to do with it.**

A code point can prove that two characters are different.

It cannot tell us whether we should make them the same.

That decision depends on the purpose of the dataset.

Do not make the mistake of assuming that every real difference should be removed.

Some differences may carry useful information.

Others may be accidental inconsistencies.

We inspect first so that our choices are deliberate.

At this point, we have not trained an LLM.

We have not created tokens, token IDs, or embeddings

We have prepared a more consistent piece of source text that later stages can work with.

That prepared text becomes a dependable starting point for the next lesson, where we will begin moving from complete text towards the smaller pieces that an LLM can process.